{
	title: 'Web input as 4s specifies: cursor, touch Enter, typing anywhere, phone keyboard',
	spec: 'The web client meets the requirements task 4s gained after it was built (see its description and artifacts old-cursor-in-card.diff, old-viewport.ts): the cursor shows inside the card that is streaming; on a touch keyboard (pointer: coarse) Enter inserts a newline and sending is the send button; typing a printable key anywhere on the page goes to the prompt without losing the key; the page is sized from visualViewport so the iOS keyboard never hides the prompt or scrolls the page behind it. src/web/app.ts is at the 400-line limit: split it by job first (decisions stay in plain .ts modules with unit tests). Checked by hand in a real browser at phone and desktop widths as tasks/README.md says; CDP tests assert structure only. The composer exposes both busy-turn actions as touch buttons: Steer submits into the current turn, Queue submits with queue:true for after the turn. When not busy the primary button says Send and Queue is absent. Both share existing draft/upload and submission handling, disable on an empty prompt or open question, preserve focus, and have at least 44px touch targets without squeezing the message field to nothing on phones. The textarea is capped in CSS at 40% of the live visual viewport, so opening the keyboard clamps even an unchanged draft rather than trusting iOS layout innerHeight. The app flex containers cannot overflow into a second page scroller. Bottom safe-area padding accounts for space already hidden by the keyboard, rather than reserving the home-indicator inset again above it. Keyboard viewport correction sizes and translates the app container rather than changing the layout top of a fixed body underneath Safari focus scrolling. The document geometry stays still, the app fills the visual viewport, and the tab strip stays at its visible top while the transcript alone scrolls. Waiting/advisory floods never scroll the tab strip or composer away. The fixed prompt/status area is not a page-scroll surface: dragging static status or send/attach controls cannot pan the keyboard-visible viewport. Long drafts retain native vertical textarea scrolling and pinch zoom, but reaching either edge does not chain or bounce the page. The document rejects overscroll too, so a footer gesture cannot start repeated visual-viewport correction and trembling. The app follows all four visual-viewport bounds, including width and horizontal offset during accessible page pinch/panning; no scale multiplier is applied because these bounds already use CSS pixels. Its tab layout reflows into that visible width rather than leaving navigation cropped off-screen.',
	status: 'done',
